The formulae employed in the reduction of the azimuth observations were as follows. In the spherical triangle <math>PZS</math>, in which <math>P</math> is the pole, <math>Z</math> the zenith and <math>S</math> the star, the co-latitude <math>PZ</math> and the polar distance <math>PS</math> are known, and, as the angle at <math>S</math> is a right angle at the elongation, the hour angle and the azimuth at that time are found from the equations
{{block center|<math>\cos P=\tan PS\cot PZ,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\cos Z=\cos PS\sin P.</math>}}
The interval, <math>\delta P</math>, between the time of any observation and that of the elongation being known, the corresponding azimuthal angle, <math>\delta Z</math>, between the two positions of the star at the times of observation and elongation is given rigorously by the following expression —<math>\tan\delta Z</math>
{{block center|<math>=-\frac{2\sin^2\tfrac{1}{2}\delta P}{\cot PS\sin PZ\sin P\{1+\tan^2 PS\cos\delta P+\sec^2 PS\cot P\sin\delta P\}}</math> (13),}}
which is expressed as follows for logarithmic computation—
{{block center|<math>\delta Z=-\frac{m\tan Z\cos^2 PS}{1-n+l},</math>}}
where <math>m=2\sin^2\frac{\delta P}{2}\operatorname{cosec}1''</math>, <math>n=2\sin^2 PS\sin^2\frac{\delta P}{2}</math>, and <math>l=\cot P\sin\delta P</math>; <math>l</math>, <math>m</math>, and <math>n</math> are tabulated.

