''Height and Refraction.'' Let <math>A</math> and <math>B</math> (fig. 4) be any two points t at <math>C</math>, cutting the sea-level at <math>p</math> and <math>q</math>; ta the difference of height; draw the tangents <math>Aa</math> and <math>Bb</math> at <math>A</math> and <math>B</math>, then <math>aAB</math> is the depression of <math>B</math> at <math>A</math> and <math>bBA</math> that of <math>A</math> at <math>B</math>; join <math>AD</math>, then <math>BD</math> is determined from the triangle <math>ABD</math>. The triangulation gives the distance between <math>A</math> and <math>B</math> at the sea-level, whence <math>pq=c</math>; thus, putting <math>Ap</math>, the height of <math>A</math> above the sea-level, <math>=H</math>, and <math>pC=r</math>,
{{block center|<math>AD = c\left(1 + \frac{H}{r} - \frac{c^2}{24r^2}\right)</math> (14).}}
Putting <math>D_a</math> and <math>D_b</math> for the actual depressions at <math>A</math> and <math>B</math>, <math>S</math> for the angle at <math>A</math>, usually called the "subtended angle," and <math>h</math> for <math>BD</math>—
{{block center|<math>S = \tfrac{1}{2}(D_b - D_a)</math> (15),}}
{{block center|and <math>h = AD\frac{\sin S}{\cos D_b}</math> (16).}}
